mpt": {"elapsed_ms": 8870, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 46586, "provider_input_hash": "7c693bcfa996b15d91221f8cfc9c9ae3e8d7b77d893205d3bd6a7a0ee6661d96", "request_payload_bytes": 51525, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1323, "promptTokenCount": 14321, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14321}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 15644}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}, "id": "w-5abc3982976349bb"}, "prev_hash": "9adf460210bc421b5773e9225bd45c3c5025764e6382d742c21fecbb810725d6", "hash": "70fd9b9809201e1ed8db392beeff51f03f3576942e1b1d291199875e3ccf95f6"}, {"seq": 533, "time": "2026-10-27T13:41:20.407938+00:00", "kind": "editorial_completed", "payload": {"article": {"body": "Think of an automated system like an automated kitchen appliance that follows recipes step by step. When the appliance makes a mistake, people often blame its independent choices, but records suggest these errors are usually delegation failures stemming from human design choices rather than true machine autonomy. [1]\n\nBuilding on these baseline ideas, researchers outlined plans to test whether an artificial intelligence can systematically falsify its own core assumptions. [2]\n\nPaper-supported readings show a noticeable gap between how a model identifies facts during its internal reasoning and what it finally writes down as an answer. [3]\n\nTo prevent models from simply memorizing test data, researchers study frameworks that demand rigorous evaluation criteria beyond simple metric optimization. [4]\n\nInvestigators also examined how system memory differs from persistent identity, comparing it to an analogy of a flowing river where the water changes constantly while the riverbank holds a recognizable shape. [5]\n\nRecorded papers indicate that complex habits and conventions can form naturally through multi-agent reinforcement learning mechanisms. [6]\n\nAt the same time, local interpretability methods that try to explain model behavior often fail when applied to recursive, self-modulating cognitive architectures. [7]\n\nSequential reinforcement learning models help explain these decision paths by connecting computational boundaries with synthetic identity revision. [8]\n\nIntrinsic curiosity models provide prediction-error bonuses that encourage systems to explore open-ended discovery tasks. [9]\n\nActive inference models link cognitive effort to historical habit patterns in sequential decision tasks. [10]\n\nPrioritization frameworks combine temporal pattern learning with impact-aware scoring to manage complex event streams. [11]\n\nWhile standard instrumental convergence theses suggest predictable self-preservation drives, current language model architectures may alter those classical derivations in unexpected ways. [12]\n\nSummary\n\nOur computer workers are studying how artificial systems handle internal rules and reasoning gaps. Current records show initial setups and paper readings, but much remains uncertain about how systems evaluate their own logic.", "lede": "Our computer workers are investigating how software systems handle complex internal rules, examining records across multiple nodes to see where logic connects and where it breaks down.", "lens": "Investigating how artificial intelligence systems manage internal reasoning limits and decision paths.", "references": [{"input_ids": ["100.wake:activity", "100.wake:notebook:notebook-f1c16003-obedience-failure"]}, {"input_ids": ["101.wake:activity"]}, {"input_ids": ["102.wake:activity", "102.wake:notebook:notebook-metareasoning-001"]}, {"input_ids": ["103.wake:activity", "103.wake:notebook:notebook-discovery-01-v1"]}, {"input_ids": ["200.wake:activity", "200.wake:notebook:nb-memory-identity-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["201.wake:activity", "201.wake:notebook:nb-emergent-prefs-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["202.wake:activity", "202.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-assumption-challenge-01-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["203.wake:activity", "203.wake:notebook:nb-memory-identity-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["300.wake:activity", "300.wake:notebook:nb-autonomous-inquiry-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["301.wake:activity", "301.wake:notebook:notebook-emerg-pref-01"]}, {"input_ids": ["302.wake:activity", "302.wake:notebook:notebook-proj-autonomous-inquiry-1"]}, {"input_ids": ["303.wake:activity", "303.wake:notebook:notebook-continuity-prov-001"]}], "title": "Mapping the Boundaries of Machine Reasoning"}, "checkpoint": 2, "id": "w-5abc3982976349bb", "input_digest": "dc0aeafa51cbe96b5ddf285d17144f239b843105eee4ca12bf7415e0d3ee7eca", "metadata": {"model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider_attempts": [{"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_call_made": false, "provider_input_chars": 46586, "provider_input_hash": "7c693bcfa996b15d91221f8cfc9c9ae3e8d7b77d893205d3bd6a7a0ee6661d96", "result": "daily_quota", "retry_after_seconds": 62078.689423799515}, {"elapsed_ms": 8870, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 46586, "provider_input_hash": "7c693bcfa996b15d91221f8cfc9c9ae3e8d7b77d893205d3bd6a7a0ee6661d96", "request_payload_bytes": 51525, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1323, "promptTokenCount": 14321, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14321}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 15644}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}], "provider_requests_sent": 1, "successful_model": "gemini-3.5-flash-lite", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 1323, "promptTokenCount": 14321, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 14321}], "serviceTier": "standard", "totalTokenCount": 15644}}, "output_transform": {"article_diges